###################  Part 1: Top 20 Basic-Level Questions  #####################

1. What is Spring Boot and how is it different from the Spring Framework?
Spring Boot is built on top of the core Spring Framework. It removes boilerplate by providing auto-configuration,
starter dependencies, and an embedded server, so you get a production-ready app with minimal XML/manual config.
Plain Spring requires you to wire beans, configure the DispatcherServlet, and deploy a WAR yourself.

2. What is Inversion of Control (IoC) and Dependency Injection (DI)?
IoC means the framework — not your code — controls object creation and wiring.
DI is the mechanism: Spring creates objects (beans) and injects their dependencies via constructor, setter, or field injection,
instead of the object creating its own dependencies with new.

3. What is the Spring IoC container / ApplicationContext?
It's the container that manages the full lifecycle of beans — creation, dependency resolution, initialization, and destruction.
ApplicationContext is the advanced container (over BeanFactory) with event propagation, AOP integration, and internationalization support.

4. Difference between @Component, @Service, @Repository, @Controller?
All are stereotypes of @Component and get auto-detected by component scanning.
@Service marks business logic (semantic only),
@Repository additionally enables Spring's exception translation (DB exceptions → DataAccessException), and
@Controller/@RestController marks web layer beans (the latter adds @ResponseBody to every method).

5. What is @SpringBootApplication?
A meta-annotation combining @Configuration (marks it a config class),
@EnableAutoConfiguration (triggers auto-config based on classpath), and
@ComponentScan (scans the package and sub-packages for beans).

6. What are Spring Boot starters?
Curated dependency descriptors (e.g., spring-boot-starter-web, spring-boot-starter-data-jpa) that pull in a compatible,
tested set of libraries for a feature, so you don't manually manage version conflicts.

7. Constructor injection vs field injection — which is preferred and why?
Constructor injection is preferred: it makes dependencies explicit and final, enables immutability,
fails fast at startup if a bean is missing, and makes unit testing easy without Spring (just call the constructor).
Field injection hides dependencies and needs reflection/Spring context for tests.

8. What is @Autowired?
Marks a dependency for Spring to inject automatically, by type (falling back to name if multiple candidates exist).
Optional on constructors since Spring 4.3 if there's only one constructor.

9. What are Spring Bean scopes?
singleton (default, one instance per container),
prototype (new instance every request), and web-aware scopes request, session, application.

10. What is application.properties / application.yml used for?
External configuration — DB URLs, server port, logging levels, custom app properties — kept outside code so
behavior can change per environment without recompiling.

11. What are Spring Profiles?
A way to segregate configuration per environment (dev, qa, prod) using application-{profile}.yml and activating one
via spring.profiles.active, so beans/config load conditionally per environment.

12. What is Spring Boot Actuator?
A set of production-ready endpoints (/actuator/health, /metrics, /env, /beans) for monitoring and managing a running application
 — health checks, metrics export, thread dumps, etc.

13. Difference between @RequestMapping and @GetMapping/@PostMapping?
@GetMapping etc. are shortcut, HTTP-method-specific versions of @RequestMapping(method = ...), introduced for readability.

14. What is @RestController?
Combines @Controller and @ResponseBody — every method's return value is serialized directly to the response body (typically JSON)
 instead of resolving to a view name.

15. How does Spring Boot handle exceptions globally?
Via @ControllerAdvice combined with @ExceptionHandler methods — centralizes exception-to-HTTP-response mapping
across all controllers instead of try/catch in every method.

16. What is Spring Data JPA and what problem does it solve?
An abstraction over JPA/Hibernate that lets you define repository interfaces (JpaRepository<Entity, ID>) and
 get CRUD + query methods generated automatically, avoiding boilerplate DAO code.

17. What is the difference between JPA, Hibernate, and Spring Data JPA?
JPA is a specification (interfaces/annotations). Hibernate is the most common implementation of that spec (does the actual ORM work).
Spring Data JPA is a Spring abstraction on top of JPA that generates repository implementations.

18. What is @Transactional?
Declares that a method should run inside a database transaction — Spring wraps it in a proxy that begins a transaction on entry
and commits/rolls back on exit (rollback by default on unchecked exceptions).

19. How do you handle validation in Spring Boot?
Using @Valid/@Validated on controller method parameters combined with Bean Validation annotations (@NotNull, @Size, @Email)
on the DTO fields; validation errors are caught via MethodArgumentNotValidException in a @ControllerAdvice.

20. What is the difference between @ComponentScan and @Import?
@ComponentScan auto-discovers annotated classes (@Component, etc.) in specified packages.
@Import explicitly registers specific @Configuration classes or beans, giving fine-grained control without scanning.


###################  Part 2: Top 20 Senior/Staff-Level Questions — Answers  ###################

1. Bean lifecycle in detail
 Instantiate → populate properties (DI) → BeanNameAware/BeanFactoryAware callbacks → BeanPostProcessor.postProcessBeforeInitialization →
 @PostConstruct / InitializingBean.afterPropertiesSet() → custom init-method → BeanPostProcessor.postProcessAfterInitialization
 (this is where AOP proxies get created) → bean ready for use → on shutdown: @PreDestroy / DisposableBean.destroy().
 BeanFactoryPostProcessor runs before any bean is instantiated and can modify bean definitions (metadata);
 BeanPostProcessor runs after instantiation and works on bean instances (e.g., wrapping them in proxies).

2. Circular dependency resolution
 Spring resolves setter/field-injection circular deps using a 3-level cache (singleton objects, early singleton objects, singleton factories)
 — it exposes a half-initialized bean early via ObjectFactory so the other bean can inject a reference to it before it's fully populated.
 Constructor injection can't do this because both beans need a fully-constructed instance of each other at construction time —
 there's no "early reference" to hand out — so Spring throws BeanCurrentlyInCreationException.

3. @Component vs @Bean
 @Component is class-level, for classes you own — detected via classpath scanning.
 @Bean is method-level inside a @Configuration class, used when you need to instantiate a class you don't own (third-party library)
 or need custom construction logic that annotations can't express.

4. Bean scope proxy problem
 Injecting a prototype/request-scoped bean directly into a singleton freezes it at the first-resolved instance since
 the singleton is only wired once. Fix: inject a scoped proxy (proxyMode = ScopedProxyMode.TARGET_CLASS) so every method call
 is routed through the proxy to fetch the current scoped instance, or inject an ObjectProvider<T>/Provider<T> and resolve lazily.

5. AOP proxy mechanics
 If the target class implements an interface, Spring uses a JDK dynamic proxy (implements the same interface,
 delegates via InvocationHandler). If not, it falls back to CGLIB (subclasses the target class at runtime and overrides methods).
 Self-invocation breaks @Transactional/@Async because calling this.method() from within the same class bypasses the proxy entirely
 — the proxy's advice only triggers on external calls through the bean reference.

6. Auto-configuration internals
 @EnableAutoConfiguration triggers Spring Boot to read META-INF/spring/org.springframework.boot.autoconfigure.AutoConfiguration.imports
 (pre-Boot-2.7 it was spring.factories), loading a list of @Configuration classes. Each is gated by @Conditional... annotations
 — @ConditionalOnClass (only if a class is on the classpath), @ConditionalOnMissingBean (only if you haven't defined your own),
 @ConditionalOnProperty — so config only activates when relevant and never overrides your explicit beans.

7. Writing a custom starter
 Create an autoconfigure module with a @Configuration class using @ConditionalOnClass/@ConditionalOnMissingBean to define default beans,
 bind custom properties via @ConfigurationProperties, and register the class in AutoConfiguration.imports.
 Package it as a separate -starter module that just declares the dependency, following the naming convention xxx-spring-boot-starter.

8. Config precedence Highest to lowest (roughly):
 command-line args → SPRING_APPLICATION_JSON env var → JNDI/Servlet params → System.getProperties() → OS env vars →
 profile-specific application-{profile}.yml (external, then packaged) → application.yml (external, then packaged) →
 @PropertySource → default properties. Profile-specific files always beat non-profile ones regardless of location.

9. Embedded server internals
 Boot bootstraps an embedded Tomcat/Jetty/Netty via ServletWebServerApplicationContext, which detects a ServletWebServerFactory bean
 on the classpath and starts the server programmatically instead of deploying to an external container.
 Thread pools are tuned via server.tomcat.threads.max/min-spare (Tomcat's executor) balanced against your DB connection
 pool size to avoid threads blocked waiting on a shared resource.

10. @Transactional propagation/isolation
 REQUIRED (default) joins an existing transaction or creates one; REQUIRES_NEW always suspends any existing transaction
 and starts a fresh one — useful for an audit-log write that must persist even if the outer transaction rolls back;
 NESTED creates a savepoint within the existing transaction, allowing partial rollback to that point without aborting the whole transaction.
 Isolation levels (READ_COMMITTED, REPEATABLE_READ, SERIALIZABLE) control what concurrent transactions can see of each other's
 uncommitted/committed changes — e.g., use SERIALIZABLE for a seat-booking system to prevent double-booking, at the cost of throughput.

11. Self-invocation and @Transactional
 Same root cause as AOP proxy mechanics (Q5) — since @Transactional is itself a proxy-based advice, calling the annotated method
 from another method in the same class skips the proxy and runs the raw method, so no transaction is opened. Fix: inject a self-reference
 (ApplicationContext.getBean(this.getClass())) or, cleaner, move the transactional method into a separate bean and call it from there.

12. Hibernate caching & N+1
 First-level cache is the Session/EntityManager-scoped cache — always on, cleared per transaction. Second-level cache is SessionFactory-scoped
 (shared across sessions, needs an external provider like Ehcache/Redis), caching entities across transactions. N+1 happens when fetching a
 parent triggers one query, then a separate query per child on lazy access — fixed with JOIN FETCH in JPQL, @EntityGraph to eagerly
 fetch specific associations, or batch fetching (@BatchSize) to group the N queries into fewer IN-clause queries.

13. Optimistic vs pessimistic locking
 Optimistic locking (@Version column) assumes low contention — it checks the version number on update and throws OptimisticLockException
 if it changed, letting you retry or surface a conflict to the user; good for high-read, low-write scenarios like editable profiles.
 Pessimistic locking (SELECT ... FOR UPDATE via @Lock(PESSIMISTIC_WRITE)) actually locks the row at read time, blocking other
 transactions — used for high-contention writes like inventory decrement where retries are wasteful. Handle OptimisticLockException
 by catching it at the service layer, reloading the entity, and either retrying the update or returning a 409 Conflict to the caller.

14. Thread pool sizing
 For CPU-bound work, pool size ≈ number of cores (+1) — more threads just cause context-switch overhead.
 For I/O-bound work (DB calls, external APIs), size using threads = cores * (1 + wait_time/compute_time) — since threads spend most time
 blocked waiting, you can run far more than core count concurrently. In practice, tune based on load-testing against real latency numbers,
 not just the formula.

15. Request concurrency & virtual threads
 Traditional Spring MVC is thread-per-request on the servlet container's platform-thread pool — a blocking call (DB, HTTP) ties up an OS
 thread for the whole duration, so scaling concurrent requests means scaling OS threads, which is expensive. With virtual threads
 (spring.threads.virtual.enabled=true on Boot 3.2+/Java 21), each request gets a cheap virtual thread; when it blocks on I/O,
 the underlying carrier OS thread is freed to run other virtual threads — so you get massively higher concurrency for the same blocking code,
 without switching to reactive/WebFlux.

16. Caching strategy / @Cacheable
 @Cacheable wraps the method in a proxy (same AOP mechanism as @Transactional) — on call, it checks the configured CacheManager
 for a key (default: method args) and returns the cached value if present, otherwise runs the method and stores the result.
 @CacheEvict/@CachePut manage invalidation/updates. For a distributed cache with Redis, back the CacheManager with RedisCacheManager,
 set a sane TTL, and design keys carefully to avoid stampedes (consider cache-aside with a short lock or request coalescing for hot keys).

17. Resilience patterns
 Circuit breaker (Resilience4j @CircuitBreaker) stops calling a failing downstream service after a failure threshold, failing fast instead
 of piling up blocked calls that could exhaust your own thread pool — protects against cascading failure. Retry re-attempts a
 transient failure (network blip) with backoff — protects against short-lived glitches, but must be idempotent-safe or it can amplify
 load on an already-struggling service. Rate limiting caps outbound/inbound call rate to protect a downstream dependency (or yourself) from
 being overwhelmed — a different failure mode than the other two (self-protection vs resilience to failure).

18. Idempotency & distributed transactions
 Idempotent APIs use a client-supplied idempotency key stored server-side (or natural idempotency like PUT with a full resource state)
 so a retried request doesn't duplicate the effect. For cross-service consistency without 2PC (which needs a blocking coordinator and
 doesn't scale well), use the Saga pattern: each service commits its local transaction and publishes an event; if a later step fails,
 compensating transactions undo prior steps — trading strong consistency for eventual consistency and availability.

19. Spring Security filter chain
 A request passes through an ordered chain of servlet filters (SecurityFilterChain) — e.g., CorsFilter → CsrfFilter → authentication filters
 (UsernamePasswordAuthenticationFilter or a custom JWT filter) → ExceptionTranslationFilter → FilterSecurityInterceptor/AuthorizationFilter
 for the final access decision. For stateless JWT auth: a custom OncePerRequestFilter extracts and validates the token, builds an Authentication
 object, and sets it on SecurityContextHolder — with SessionCreationPolicy.STATELESS so Spring never creates an HttpSession.

20. Observability setup
 Actuator exposes /health, /metrics, /info, etc. out of the box; Micrometer is the metrics facade behind it, exporting to backends like
 Prometheus/Datadog with dimensional tags. Distributed tracing (Spring Cloud Sleuth historically, now Micrometer Tracing + OpenTelemetry)
 propagates a trace ID/span ID across service calls via headers, so a request can be followed end-to-end across microservices.
 For structured logging, use a JSON log encoder (Logback + logstash-encoder) and inject the trace/correlation ID into the MDC so
 every log line across services can be correlated by request.